PRINTF(3)                 Programmer's Manual                PRINTF(3)

NAME
       _printf - print formatted output to the standard output.

SYNOPSIS
       #include "main.h"

       int _printf(const char *format, ...);

DESCRIPTION
       _printf writes formatted output to the standard output. It mimics
       the basic behavior of the standard printf(3) function, but supports
       only a limited set of format specifiers.

       The format string may contain:
         • ordinary characters, printed as-is
         • conversion specifiers beginning with '%' that determine how
           additional arguments are formatted

SUPPORTED SPECIFIERS
       %c      Print a single character.
       %s      Print a null-terminated string.
       %d, %i  Print a signed integer.
       %%      Print a literal percent sign.

PARAMETERS
       format  A string containing ordinary characters and format
               specifiers. If format is NULL, the behavior is undefined.

       ...     Additional arguments matching the format specifiers.

RETURN VALUE
       Returns the total number of characters printed, or -1 if an error
       occurs (NULL format, unsupported specifier, write failure).

ERRORS
       -1 is returned if:
         • format is NULL
         • an unsupported format specifier is encountered
         • a write error occurs

EXAMPLES
       _printf("Hello %s\n", "world");
           Output: Hello world

       _printf("Value: %d\n", 42);
           Output: Value: 42

       _printf("Percent: %%\n");
           Output: Percent: %

SEE ALSO
       printf(3), putchar(3), va_list(3), variadic functions.

